WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.160
If the creators of "American Pickers" had it&nbsp;
their way, viewers would feel as if everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.160
about the show is authentic and impromptu. When&nbsp;
it comes to finding and buying interesting items&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.348
from collectors across the country, the show&nbsp;
frames it as if everything is spontaneous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.348 --> 00:00:15.040
The show's star pickers - or at&nbsp;
least the original star pickers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.000
Frank and Mike, either spot something&nbsp;
that piques their interest on the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.317
or their storefront superstar, Danielle, calls&nbsp;
them with a tip on a potential picking place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:26.389
Regardless of the method, Frank and Mike's&nbsp;
picking journey across America looks natural.

00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:29.840
Of course, as with all reality shows,&nbsp;
there's a little more orchestration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.600
behind the camera than the show's producers&nbsp;
really want fans to know. Take a touch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.760
reality away from the reality show, and it&nbsp;
becomes much more entertaining. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.798
you might not be surprised to learn that Frank&nbsp;
and Mike aren't always the ones who do the picking&nbsp;on "American Pickers." 

00:00:42.798 --> 00:00:45.608
On top of that, they&nbsp;
might not even handle much of the negotiating.

00:00:45.608 --> 00:00:49.280
No matter the TV show, there's always a great&nbsp;
deal of preparation that goes behind every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.840
episode prior to filming, and reality shows are&nbsp;
no exception. Even if a person's behaviors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.000
reactions are authentic and unscripted, someone&nbsp;
has probably put a ton of effort into making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.840
sure the episode follows a certain guideline.&nbsp;
In the case of "American Pickers," that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.534
that many locations, collections, and items&nbsp;
are decided long before Frank and Mike even get there. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:10.000
This is not a huge secret, either.&nbsp;
Part of the process is scouting out areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.108
and sending public calls to action to find&nbsp;
unique collections and interesting locations.

00:01:14.108 --> 00:01:17.520
According to a Go San Angelo article&nbsp;
about a local store featured on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.800
there's a long process that sellers go&nbsp;
through before anyone from "American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.080
Pickers" even goes to the chosen&nbsp;
location to look around. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.240
the store in question was Ken's Toys. The&nbsp;
owner, Ken Young, explained that he had "a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.856
three-week lead" before the production crew&nbsp;
showed up. Before that, the producers have, "...a 'snoop.' 

00:01:32.856 --> 00:01:36.320
They get somebody to go into a site&nbsp;
and look around. The man who came in that day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.917
he gave me his card. Then I got a phone call&nbsp;
and they said when they would be coming."

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:41.760
Young and his family stated that Mike and everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.760
involved in the show were&nbsp;
"knowledgeable" and kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.834
and they were really interested in the stories&nbsp;
behind the items, which Young was happy to tell.

00:01:47.834 --> 00:01:51.040
There's nothing casual about the pickers&nbsp;
process, and there's a lot of earnest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.720
effort put into finding interesting locations&nbsp;
and objects to be featured on the show. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.107
if you think that the producer's job stops at&nbsp;
finding collections, however, you'd be wrong.

00:01:59.107 --> 00:02:02.800
In addition to scouting certain collections and&nbsp;
items, the producers actually do a fair amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.921
of the negotiations. Not to discredit the picking&nbsp;
skills of Frank and Mike, who truly are the real&nbsp;deal. 

00:02:07.921 --> 00:02:12.480
However, producers often negotiate prices&nbsp;
beforehand, according to Living101. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.000
Frank and Mike have been known to continue&nbsp;
haggling after the seller and producers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.320
agreed on a price. As it's their job, they always&nbsp;
want to ensure that they can get the best profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.301
margin for the sale, and it's an aspect of the&nbsp;
business that makes the show feel more real.

00:02:24.301 --> 00:02:28.688
It makes perfect sense that the production team&nbsp;
would be skilled at finding and negotiating sales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.688 --> 00:02:33.040
The responsibility can't be completely on Frank&nbsp;
and Mike, and in reality, if the two men were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.734
only ones doing any picking, "American Pickers"&nbsp;
would move at a much slower pace than it does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:41.200
Regardless, the entire process might be&nbsp;
something to consider next time you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.240
Mike or Frank "randomly" discover a great item in&nbsp;
a collection they supposedly just stumbled upon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.160
In other words, the “reality” of this&nbsp;
reality show is a bit pre-planned.

